#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068697 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068549 :question/id #uuid "01bca086-0ed4-5626-a357-e0724685f144" :question/math-academy-id "e-3569" :question/problem "![](images/18/1867a80955ae4b456cbcb011eefed96431f2b12926994942613eb947e55e38c6.png)\n\n \n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\n\n- $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\, 2}f(x)$ exists\n\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(2)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. From the graph, we can see that the left and right-sided limits are different:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\, 2}f(x)$ does not exist.\n\n- Statement III is false.  The graph of the function breaks at $x=2.$ It's impossible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper. Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=2.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069366 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d7e85ee-6515-5fb8-a5a2-074cbc9108df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327048} {:answer/id #uuid "29de8dce-e7c7-5fc2-94a7-5179e0c2f602" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327294} {:answer/id #uuid "440c0fb1-d639-5470-acec-fe52787433f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327530} {:answer/id #uuid "937287c6-1b15-5c81-835f-d6dddc1cd449" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328220} {:answer/id #uuid "fe29cc74-1bf5-5fbc-b9ad-88af91611a92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d7e85ee-6515-5fb8-a5a2-074cbc9108df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327048} :answer-field/id #uuid "62261799-63f8-547f-a1a7-46cbd5868baa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329357}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "544abde9-c223-5348-b980-33df85581d2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-48042" :question/problem "![](images/8e/8eb53506d9e01d09fe3e072a2c6ed1ade05e1cc185693bdd66b34bdf75797ad9.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. \n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)$ is finite\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)$ is finite\n- $f(x)$  has a discontinuity at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is false. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is true. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function contains a break exactly at $x=2$ and it's impossible to draw its graph without taking our pencil off of the paper."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069367 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a26128fd-a27e-5a35-8575-639ff9311589" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067373} {:answer/id #uuid "b3835136-311e-52c3-8f9d-f243c85a4875" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067374} {:answer/id #uuid "c7f192a9-8305-5e68-97be-10e4dac05d62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067375} {:answer/id #uuid "330a48ae-091d-566c-827d-bd6e6174a48a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067376} {:answer/id #uuid "5b7c3791-ee87-5538-8024-8ad1d642d14a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067377}] :answer-field/id #uuid "1a24ceb9-b141-5695-8ef8-3816685f8054" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067372}] :question/id #uuid "a302c934-1e7e-57a4-882d-8cd125952f61" :question/math-academy-id "q-48064" :question/problem "![](images/02/0218a1e13a0504b20e802100f9e15877beeb2b565a49fe7cd01cf2a0cf0197ee.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f(x)$ is defined at $x = 2$\n2. $lim_(x → 2)f(x)$ exists\n3. $f(x)$ is continuous at $x = 2$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234087 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2832c750-5223-5055-a543-69f3bfc7bf6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-48063" :question/problem "![](images/f5/f5ba5f7cc2ea5aaf405db1abff7e6f28ed4378ba6a5ec88d73c6861ede1d75de.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. - $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\nII. - ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)$ exists\nIII. - $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(2)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The graph of $f(x)$ breaks at $x=2$,  it's impossible to draw the graph without taking our pencil off of the paper."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240786 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97842440-8562-5084-8515-ac9bed4f30cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239366} {:answer/id #uuid "adf67485-98f6-5532-b74e-ad997abb4115" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239564} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f0ae0b-0e27-5e62-ba20-05fe58021513" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239664} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1ddd11-773f-5bba-9c4f-50345066fd1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239859} {:answer/id #uuid "edb1bc67-aa4a-5b31-9afb-59791c7d655e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edb1bc67-aa4a-5b31-9afb-59791c7d655e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240149} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b0cf691-b10b-512c-8716-cad8675c07cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240407}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "340a8e11-ca0d-5e08-ab08-b9647c00e4dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-104049" :question/problem "![](images/51/5143df65d0f99c6511dc8ab13262831669c88535e19c986dacdb364e04847f58.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)\\ne f(2)$\n- $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. We have $f(2)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=2=f(2).$\n\n- Statement III is false. The graph of the function doesn't have a break at $x=2$ and we can draw the graph of $f(x)$ around $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240787 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e8bd6dd-1931-5df3-803b-dc0e5167142e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238463} {:answer/id #uuid "4d8ed7c5-b2af-580b-8113-c798acb35065" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238721} {:answer/id #uuid "92b58f29-e9d2-52ea-80a1-a85417e0693d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239321} {:answer/id #uuid "d6b9cbc0-4187-50a5-81fd-c6539f6fe9ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239932} {:answer/id #uuid "f9aeed86-3876-53b5-b2e9-7b048f60598b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9aeed86-3876-53b5-b2e9-7b048f60598b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240264} :answer-field/id #uuid "42c90896-bfe8-504a-9692-0915b93a4eb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240445}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc9393f2-809c-5769-b329-186102da51c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-104051" :question/problem "![](images/65/65b9b32e8fac6a71141ce820855058367e9235129de7334db35a200905474cc0.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $f(x)$ is defined at $x=5$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,5}f(x)$ exists\n\nIII. $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=5$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(5)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{+}}f(x) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,  ${lim}_{x\\to \\,5}f(x)$ doesn't exist\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of the function has a jump exactly at $x=5$\nand it's impossible to draw the graph without taking our pencil off of the paper."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240788 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "013b8848-3885-5ddc-abba-c6d98f5249e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238091} {:answer/id #uuid "60b0fbff-7602-5867-b0a7-49ff7cf237ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238904} {:answer/id #uuid "ac75e25e-87f7-560b-989e-a0db688ca52b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239554} {:answer/id #uuid "c1fdba29-79ec-5716-8a2d-1c3ed48f88b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239755} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb22f50-3d68-5e9a-9196-af09faba8f5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240130}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60b0fbff-7602-5867-b0a7-49ff7cf237ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238904} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9e6d376-017a-5f97-89b1-398a2e796dd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240658}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3312f9fe-c2a1-5d75-b752-6c981a0a3f4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-104052" :question/problem "![](images/80/8086893e4587e68f8257b75f2070cf622fa141199d6bd8960f7c4fd8e7bcb349.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is **not** defined at $x=0$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)$ exists\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph we can see that no value has been assigned to $f(x)$ at $x=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x) & =3, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x) & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function is not even defined at $x=0.$ The graph contains a break at $x=0.$ It's impossible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240800 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d0261a4-d877-5906-ad0b-830747a5cc4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239135} {:answer/id #uuid "8ad4d9e3-4681-5c2a-91a3-0ffe435a5b1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239261} {:answer/id #uuid "b164497e-772a-5260-941e-feac495b0767" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239604} {:answer/id #uuid "bb707977-2299-58a7-be64-009156d71aed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239688} {:answer/id #uuid "e7c3ed1b-b297-5c48-93a5-4e4568f8c09c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb707977-2299-58a7-be64-009156d71aed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239688} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0db112b-2842-5c5e-b64c-f115b941ce9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240639}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ad98348d-cbe6-535a-9828-f9008c2ec2e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-104124" :question/problem "![](images/bd/bdcfa35fb04028cbff32992b41214c878443d3815b676a0cf046f5a99bd771bd.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is **not** defined at $x=-2$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=f(-2)$\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-2$ and $f(-2)=5.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x) & =5, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x) & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=5=f(-2).$\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of $f(x)$ can be drawn without taking our pencil off of the paper in the neighborhood of $x=-2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329853 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19eb6e8d-8d23-5950-9d1f-719568b31714" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327158} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc07d22-ae7a-5837-bd01-69ca3c1713a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328314} {:answer/id #uuid "cca94791-290b-5089-b6f7-05b72b8ffcf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328728} {:answer/id #uuid "d5090347-0ef4-5430-bfdb-5390e21fb3ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328798} {:answer/id #uuid "f1f1caf8-4682-580c-b7d8-ee546c5a8d5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5090347-0ef4-5430-bfdb-5390e21fb3ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328798} :answer-field/id #uuid "78f6c570-d96e-503c-820b-a483ee09771d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329384}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "18223e97-17ad-509c-a688-bf8d869b747a" :question/math-academy-id "q-48040" :question/problem "![](images/59/59c02273515e87fcebb8eb1785dc2cf0c6490feda4fc9644df43b8849921eec5.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-1$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)$ exists\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-1$ and we have $f(-1)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, both left-hand side and right-hand side limits exist but they are not the same. Therefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)$ does not exist.\n\n- Statement III is false. The function contains a break exactly at $x=-1$ and it's impossible to draw its graph without taking our pencil off of the paper."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329854 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ee867b9-645a-512d-9f99-f3aa8226ad71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327063} {:answer/id #uuid "4558acbf-a492-5e1c-acde-0aa26d25d7f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327540} {:answer/id #uuid "a58a5f87-8e65-5592-8409-a19f0dfcd0ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328364} {:answer/id #uuid "b013ab09-8aad-54e4-9a17-87cdc1aa5d04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328460} {:answer/id #uuid "c6636203-3372-5d9c-9639-986cdeaf7e30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ee867b9-645a-512d-9f99-f3aa8226ad71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327063} :answer-field/id #uuid "e45de7c6-c5ce-5b9d-a7b8-ea5254d09158" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329581}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "96069899-bca3-51de-b64c-6e29072ea13e" :question/math-academy-id "q-48062" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d9db7f2a491ae3065dbe8434a71d378c4fed8fec16c82e2fd76dcb209c786809.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)$ exists\n\nIII. $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is false. From the graph we can see that no value has been assigned to $f(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x) & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function is not even defined at $x=1.$ The graph contains a break at $x=1.$ It's impossible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329855 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f05bd8f-eb2f-57d7-ae18-c11817bd78b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327620} {:answer/id #uuid "83335e31-f97c-5df4-863a-ef88150fb6f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328065} {:answer/id #uuid "9a9a91aa-3ed8-5151-bcb5-e59ccd13d6bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328272} {:answer/id #uuid "ce9e8871-1f30-5744-8374-042f56698b0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328750} {:answer/id #uuid "d6472410-aa22-5e41-a0c0-09e47c3fb93c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83335e31-f97c-5df4-863a-ef88150fb6f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328065} :answer-field/id #uuid "23e84077-b88d-5119-bcf7-6c45cbfd0278" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329249}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e01edc7-98f4-52fe-bf43-cb1e05ae0d4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-48065" :question/problem "![](images/fa/fa15192923f9fd7370818fcb6b05b24b3bb6901dfbf90dd53539d71fd0f8b0bf.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=f(2)$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$ and $f(2)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=1=f(2).$\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of $f(x)$ can be drawn without taking our pencil off of the paper in the neighborhood of $x=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329856 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b74aed1-5750-566e-973a-b9fe0109c876" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327995} {:answer/id #uuid "98ce5d05-688f-5cf5-9b21-352ade35d261" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328254} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf58768-099b-5e1e-ba54-551e1eec0a5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328289} {:answer/id #uuid "b440b75f-1cc0-592d-87f4-cd361fc03170" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328501} {:answer/id #uuid "ef006457-c201-515f-919a-a5f8772d65f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b74aed1-5750-566e-973a-b9fe0109c876" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327995} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fafe6c7-ed63-54d7-8bcd-351dbdd97823" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329309}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7aef8bfd-11ab-51f5-ad27-2faedcadc29e" :question/math-academy-id "q-48067" :question/problem "![](images/c1/c132c73b7ca4759882959f9df42110c36e31f3c8af27afa02c0caa20f5f94d52.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=f(1)$\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. We have $f(1)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x) & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1=f(1).$\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of $f(x)$ can be drawn without taking our pencil off of the paper in the neighborhood of $x=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329857 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18eaadf1-683f-5951-8da7-2c8b04aa572f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327151} {:answer/id #uuid "97125a5f-4f67-5544-94dd-ed787cd4805a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328242} {:answer/id #uuid "aa7f71f4-41c8-5d9e-b405-045f8fa54bac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328409} {:answer/id #uuid "f92419cf-94b1-5b46-bc59-f5aee0d957ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329120} {:answer/id #uuid "fd8882da-83f4-5fd5-a9fa-309e57cccf12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa7f71f4-41c8-5d9e-b405-045f8fa54bac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328409} :answer-field/id #uuid "d63a5833-0377-5a91-a4df-afbabcc49049" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329564}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f506060c-db8a-5e26-aad7-74b6b6b44f61" :question/math-academy-id "q-48068" :question/problem "![](images/8a/8a3874b7907227653d93451d9ce0960fdadc8cb3e10396e0f27f7ebb363bebe2.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. \n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)$ is finite\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)$ is **not** finite\n- $f(x)$  is  continuous at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of the function doesn't have a break at $x=-1$ and we can draw the graph of $f(x)$ around $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper."}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068779 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068619 :question/id #uuid "d86c6bd2-e528-55ed-bbfc-9f5ecbec024e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5924" :question/problem "Graph the function $y = -\\dfrac{2}{x-1}-1.$" :question/worked-solution "To plot $y = -\\dfrac{2}{x-1}-1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=-\\dfrac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/9d/9da0db2e6c97e507e82b7ca801e46802fabf91ad31ff53ad3cc9ae580151370a.png)\n\n- Translate it by one unit to the right to get the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{1}{x-1}.$ \n\n![](images/8c/8ce8c53f202f04b3b34a247b797f65f7616e8772afa1751de126ddd401417bd7.png)\n\nNotice that the vertical asymptote is now $x=1.$\n\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{2}{x-1}.$\n\n![](images/c9/c9b708a20d9c6c12f0016279e8d7a3ea55597e7645f0a9518f1c32e8a8406e47.png)\n\n- Finally, translate it by $1$ unit down to get the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{2}{x-1}-1.$ \n\n![](images/e1/e100b552adb71cae1295492a58b0e60525813adb87d9815ba64a151a856f0c46.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote is now $y=-1.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4640f978-9caa-5960-bec4-f153a1b4cc5d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069100 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4675e39-a192-509f-84e6-821038b03fac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/25/2560fc488346c9fe2e89b0006cbd230681dc51cfc10eb289fe82829706f15130.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066034} {:answer/id #uuid "c89d9ffb-8f59-5730-8a76-57b0b8117be3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5d/5d24b48ce1c09f81d92619299cc44fe4cc44197a2f1fe1dacc32b8743af2e664.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066035} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd6d952-62cc-5305-8d8b-1c2e3ed65a57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5a/5a266218ee01d4d74e8765f378b1aebf1aaff3c0a2c65914d49c871655b039ad.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066036} {:answer/id #uuid "0fac3cfc-ba15-5257-89df-ab4385ec1c13" :answer/type {:db/ident :a